1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,400
Sweetheart, in December of two thousand fifteen,

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,092
I spent four months knitting a sweater

3
00:00:06,092 --> 00:00:07,650
for my grandson Tyler.

4
00:00:07,830 --> 00:00:10,350
Navy blue, with a white stripe,

5
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:11,813
his school colors.

6
00:00:11,993 --> 00:00:13,363
He was fifteen.

7
00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:15,650
And on Christmas morning,

8
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:16,830
he held it up,

9
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:18,094
and he laughed,

10
00:00:18,094 --> 00:00:19,105
and he said,

11
00:00:19,105 --> 00:00:22,308
Grandma, nobody wears stuff like this.

12
00:00:22,488 --> 00:00:24,555
His cousins laughed too.

13
00:00:24,735 --> 00:00:26,810
He left it on my couch when

14
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:27,962
they went home.

15
00:00:28,142 --> 00:00:30,580
And he never knew what I'd stitched

16
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:31,625
inside the hem.

17
00:00:31,976 --> 00:00:34,363
Tonight I'm going to tell you where

18
00:00:34,363 --> 00:00:37,024
that sweater spent the next nine years,

19
00:00:37,024 --> 00:00:39,207
and the night Tyler showed up at

20
00:00:39,207 --> 00:00:40,776
my door at twenty-four,

21
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:42,413
with nowhere else to go.

22
00:00:42,593 --> 00:00:45,212
And what he found when he finally

23
00:00:45,212 --> 00:00:46,006
put it on.

24
00:00:46,186 --> 00:00:47,154
Stay with me,

25
00:00:47,154 --> 00:00:50,205
honey, because he's sitting in my kitchen

26
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:52,363
right now, and he's knitting.

27
00:00:52,713 --> 00:00:55,446
I learned to knit from my Grandma

28
00:00:55,446 --> 00:00:57,185
Pearl when I was six,

29
00:00:57,185 --> 00:00:57,682
honey.

30
00:00:57,862 --> 00:00:59,166
And every Christmas,

31
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:01,644
for as long as I've had grandchildren,

32
00:01:01,644 --> 00:01:03,992
I've knitted something for each one.

33
00:01:04,172 --> 00:01:06,006
Mittens when they're little,

34
00:01:06,006 --> 00:01:07,840
scarves when they're bigger.

35
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:11,372
But the year each grandchild turned fifteen,

36
00:01:11,372 --> 00:01:13,048
I made them a sweater.

37
00:01:13,228 --> 00:01:14,586
A real one.

38
00:01:14,766 --> 00:01:17,082
It takes me about four months,

39
00:01:17,082 --> 00:01:19,398
now that my fingers are stiff.

40
00:01:19,578 --> 00:01:21,180
I start in August.

41
00:01:21,531 --> 00:01:23,906
Tyler is Susan's oldest boy,

42
00:01:23,906 --> 00:01:24,839
sweetheart.

43
00:01:25,019 --> 00:01:27,132
He was such a sweet little thing.

44
00:01:27,312 --> 00:01:28,538
When he was five,

45
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:30,483
he used to sit on the floor

46
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:32,429
at my feet while I knitted,

47
00:01:32,429 --> 00:01:34,736
and wind the yarn into balls for

48
00:01:34,736 --> 00:01:34,952
me.

49
00:01:35,132 --> 00:01:36,618
He called it helping.

50
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:38,480
He'd say, Grandma,

51
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:39,696
when I'm big,

52
00:01:39,696 --> 00:01:42,220
will you make me a sweater?

53
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:44,095
And I promised him.

54
00:01:44,275 --> 00:01:46,202
Every year, he asked.

55
00:01:46,382 --> 00:01:47,915
Is it this year,

56
00:01:47,915 --> 00:01:48,681
Grandma?

57
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:50,718
Am I big enough?

58
00:01:51,069 --> 00:01:53,493
But fifteen is a hard age,

59
00:01:53,493 --> 00:01:54,052
honey.

60
00:01:54,232 --> 00:01:56,689
By fifteen, Tyler had a phone,

61
00:01:56,689 --> 00:01:58,819
and friends I'd never met,

62
00:01:58,819 --> 00:02:01,112
and he didn't sit at my feet

63
00:02:01,112 --> 00:02:01,767
anymore.

64
00:02:01,947 --> 00:02:03,991
He mostly grunted at me.

65
00:02:04,171 --> 00:02:05,506
That's normal.

66
00:02:05,686 --> 00:02:08,356
I raised a teenage boy myself.

67
00:02:08,536 --> 00:02:11,272
But I still remembered that little voice

68
00:02:11,272 --> 00:02:12,913
asking, is it this year?

69
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:15,345
So I picked navy and white,

70
00:02:15,345 --> 00:02:17,263
his high school colors,

71
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:19,931
the softest wool I could afford.

72
00:02:20,111 --> 00:02:22,317
Sixty-two dollars of yarn.

73
00:02:22,497 --> 00:02:24,647
In a minute I'll tell you what

74
00:02:24,647 --> 00:02:25,794
I hid inside it.

75
00:02:26,145 --> 00:02:28,243
I knitted it in the evenings,

76
00:02:28,243 --> 00:02:30,342
honey, in Harold's old chair,

77
00:02:30,342 --> 00:02:32,948
with the television turned down low.

78
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:35,996
Every row, I thought about Tyler.

79
00:02:36,176 --> 00:02:38,085
When I did the ribbing on the

80
00:02:38,085 --> 00:02:40,324
cuffs, I thought about the time he

81
00:02:40,324 --> 00:02:42,628
caught his first fish at Clear Fork

82
00:02:42,628 --> 00:02:44,933
Lake and screamed so loud he scared

83
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:45,986
it off the hook.

84
00:02:46,166 --> 00:02:47,961
When I did the white stripe,

85
00:02:47,961 --> 00:02:50,012
I thought about his first day of

86
00:02:50,012 --> 00:02:50,845
kindergarten.

87
00:02:51,025 --> 00:02:53,797
A sweater holds every thought you knit

88
00:02:53,797 --> 00:02:54,380
into it.

89
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,060
I'll tell you about the most important

90
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:58,113
one in a minute.

91
00:02:58,463 --> 00:02:59,745
On the last night,

92
00:02:59,745 --> 00:03:01,952
before I sewed up the bottom of

93
00:03:01,952 --> 00:03:04,730
the sweater, I did something my Grandma

94
00:03:04,730 --> 00:03:05,870
Pearl taught me.

95
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:07,603
She said, Ruthie,

96
00:03:07,603 --> 00:03:10,345
always leave a message inside.

97
00:03:10,525 --> 00:03:12,509
And I took a little piece of

98
00:03:12,509 --> 00:03:13,784
white cotton tape,

99
00:03:13,784 --> 00:03:16,122
and with a needle and red thread,

100
00:03:16,122 --> 00:03:18,176
I stitched seven words on it.

101
00:03:18,356 --> 00:03:20,810
Then I folded it inside the hem,

102
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:22,267
right at the front,

103
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:24,184
where his heart would be,

104
00:03:24,184 --> 00:03:25,717
and I sewed it shut.

105
00:03:25,897 --> 00:03:27,720
Nobody could see it.

106
00:03:28,070 --> 00:03:31,422
If Grandma's story is touching your heart,

107
00:03:31,422 --> 00:03:33,577
tap the like button for me,

108
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:34,455
sweetheart.

109
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:37,863
It helps more people find these stories.

110
00:03:38,043 --> 00:03:40,438
I wrapped that sweater in red paper

111
00:03:40,438 --> 00:03:42,218
with a gold bow and put it

112
00:03:42,218 --> 00:03:44,271
under the tree with a tag that

113
00:03:44,271 --> 00:03:46,529
said, finally, you're big enough.

114
00:03:46,709 --> 00:03:48,439
Love, Grandma.

115
00:03:48,619 --> 00:03:51,046
I was so proud of that sweater.

116
00:03:51,226 --> 00:03:53,359
I'd only done it twice before,

117
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:55,208
for his two older cousins.

118
00:03:55,388 --> 00:03:57,709
This was the one he'd asked for

119
00:03:57,709 --> 00:03:59,057
since he was five.

120
00:03:59,407 --> 00:04:00,997
And Christmas morning,

121
00:04:00,997 --> 00:04:02,297
he opened it last,

122
00:04:02,297 --> 00:04:04,393
in front of the whole family,

123
00:04:04,393 --> 00:04:06,561
all the cousins in a circle on

124
00:04:06,561 --> 00:04:08,079
my living room floor.

125
00:04:08,259 --> 00:04:09,409
He held it up.

126
00:04:09,589 --> 00:04:11,462
And for one second,

127
00:04:11,462 --> 00:04:14,222
honey, I saw it on his face,

128
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:15,800
that little boy.

129
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:18,557
And then one of his cousins snickered.

130
00:04:18,737 --> 00:04:20,909
And Tyler looked at the cousins,

131
00:04:20,909 --> 00:04:22,538
and back at the sweater,

132
00:04:22,538 --> 00:04:23,555
and he laughed.

133
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:25,430
He said, Grandma,

134
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:28,321
nobody wears stuff like this.

135
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:31,357
It's like, from nineteen fifty.

136
00:04:31,708 --> 00:04:34,297
Everybody laughed, sweetheart.

137
00:04:34,477 --> 00:04:36,266
Even Susan, a little,

138
00:04:36,266 --> 00:04:38,482
before she caught herself.

139
00:04:38,662 --> 00:04:39,769
I laughed too,

140
00:04:39,769 --> 00:04:41,982
because what else do you do?

141
00:04:42,162 --> 00:04:43,278
I said, well,

142
00:04:43,278 --> 00:04:44,995
you'll grow into it.

143
00:04:45,175 --> 00:04:47,232
And I went into the kitchen to

144
00:04:47,232 --> 00:04:48,398
check on the ham,

145
00:04:48,398 --> 00:04:50,318
and I stood by the sink with

146
00:04:50,318 --> 00:04:51,552
the water running,

147
00:04:51,552 --> 00:04:53,198
so nobody could hear me.

148
00:04:53,378 --> 00:04:56,211
Only my granddaughter Lily noticed.

149
00:04:56,391 --> 00:04:57,424
She was eight.

150
00:04:57,604 --> 00:04:59,842
She came in and held my hand

151
00:04:59,842 --> 00:05:01,760
and didn't say anything.

152
00:05:02,111 --> 00:05:04,693
When they all went home that night,

153
00:05:04,693 --> 00:05:07,202
the sweater was still on my couch,

154
00:05:07,202 --> 00:05:08,751
folded, with the tag.

155
00:05:08,931 --> 00:05:10,092
He'd left it.

156
00:05:10,272 --> 00:05:11,898
Maybe by accident.

157
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:13,134
Maybe not.

158
00:05:13,314 --> 00:05:14,800
I never asked.

159
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:17,162
I wrapped it in tissue paper and

160
00:05:17,162 --> 00:05:19,071
put it in the cedar chest at

161
00:05:19,071 --> 00:05:20,366
the foot of my bed,

162
00:05:20,366 --> 00:05:23,162
with Harold's Army jacket and my mother's

163
00:05:23,162 --> 00:05:24,048
wedding veil.

164
00:05:24,228 --> 00:05:26,190
And I closed the lid.

165
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:28,548
And that was where it stayed,

166
00:05:28,548 --> 00:05:30,201
honey, for nine years.

167
00:05:30,552 --> 00:05:32,317
Some years, sweetheart,

168
00:05:32,317 --> 00:05:34,774
I'd open that cedar chest to get

169
00:05:34,774 --> 00:05:36,233
something else out,

170
00:05:36,233 --> 00:05:38,767
and I'd see the navy wool through

171
00:05:38,767 --> 00:05:40,072
the tissue paper.

172
00:05:40,252 --> 00:05:42,739
And I'd think about giving it away,

173
00:05:42,739 --> 00:05:44,800
to the church clothing drive,

174
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:47,287
to some boy who'd actually wear it.

175
00:05:47,467 --> 00:05:49,143
I almost did,

176
00:05:49,143 --> 00:05:49,917
twice.

177
00:05:50,097 --> 00:05:52,535
But something always stopped me.

178
00:05:52,715 --> 00:05:54,813
Grandma Pearl used to say,

179
00:05:54,813 --> 00:05:57,233
a gift you make for one person

180
00:05:57,233 --> 00:05:59,089
belongs to that person.

181
00:05:59,269 --> 00:06:00,500
It waits for them.

182
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:01,945
So I let it wait.

183
00:06:02,296 --> 00:06:04,648
So far, four months of knitting,

184
00:06:04,648 --> 00:06:06,046
seven secret words,

185
00:06:06,046 --> 00:06:08,178
a laugh on Christmas morning,

186
00:06:08,178 --> 00:06:10,457
and a sweater in a cedar chest.

187
00:06:10,637 --> 00:06:11,879
Tyler grew up,

188
00:06:11,879 --> 00:06:12,855
sweetheart.

189
00:06:13,035 --> 00:06:15,357
He went to college in Columbus.

190
00:06:15,537 --> 00:06:17,919
He got a job selling software,

191
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:19,190
made good money,

192
00:06:19,190 --> 00:06:21,492
married a girl named Brianna,

193
00:06:21,492 --> 00:06:23,954
bought a house with a big lawn.

194
00:06:24,134 --> 00:06:26,524
He sent me a card every Christmas,

195
00:06:26,524 --> 00:06:28,632
typed, with a photo of the two

196
00:06:28,632 --> 00:06:30,670
of them on a beach somewhere.

197
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:32,743
He never mentioned the sweater.

198
00:06:32,923 --> 00:06:34,560
And neither did I.

199
00:06:34,910 --> 00:06:37,647
And then, two thousand twenty-four,

200
00:06:37,647 --> 00:06:39,681
it all fell apart at once.

201
00:06:39,861 --> 00:06:42,087
The company let him go in the

202
00:06:42,087 --> 00:06:42,624
spring.

203
00:06:42,804 --> 00:06:44,627
Brianna left in the summer.

204
00:06:44,807 --> 00:06:46,939
They sold the house to split the

205
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:47,338
money.

206
00:06:47,518 --> 00:06:48,891
And by November,

207
00:06:48,891 --> 00:06:51,810
my grandson was living in his car,

208
00:06:51,810 --> 00:06:52,325
honey.

209
00:06:52,505 --> 00:06:54,409
He didn't tell anybody.

210
00:06:54,589 --> 00:06:55,785
Not his mother.

211
00:06:55,965 --> 00:06:56,835
Not me.

212
00:06:57,015 --> 00:06:58,432
He was too ashamed.

213
00:06:58,612 --> 00:07:00,832
He'd always been the one who had

214
00:07:00,832 --> 00:07:01,665
it together.

215
00:07:01,845 --> 00:07:02,954
Stay with me,

216
00:07:02,954 --> 00:07:05,514
because then my doorbell rang.

217
00:07:05,864 --> 00:07:08,151
I found out later that he'd parked

218
00:07:08,151 --> 00:07:10,438
outside my house three nights in a

219
00:07:10,438 --> 00:07:12,321
row before he rang the bell,

220
00:07:12,321 --> 00:07:12,725
honey.

221
00:07:12,905 --> 00:07:15,264
He'd sit there in the dark with

222
00:07:15,264 --> 00:07:16,406
the engine off,

223
00:07:16,406 --> 00:07:18,385
looking at my porch light.

224
00:07:18,565 --> 00:07:19,502
He told me,

225
00:07:19,502 --> 00:07:22,228
I kept thinking about Christmas,

226
00:07:22,228 --> 00:07:24,358
and how I laughed at you.

227
00:07:24,538 --> 00:07:26,596
I didn't think I had the right

228
00:07:26,596 --> 00:07:28,242
to ask you for anything.

229
00:07:28,422 --> 00:07:30,825
I've thought about that a lot since.

230
00:07:31,005 --> 00:07:33,434
How a little laugh at fifteen can

231
00:07:33,434 --> 00:07:35,421
keep a grown man out in the

232
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:35,788
cold.

233
00:07:36,139 --> 00:07:38,394
It was the first real snow of

234
00:07:38,394 --> 00:07:40,027
the year, sweetheart,

235
00:07:40,027 --> 00:07:41,894
eleven o'clock at night,

236
00:07:41,894 --> 00:07:44,149
the week before Thanksgiving.

237
00:07:44,329 --> 00:07:46,913
I opened the door in my bathrobe,

238
00:07:46,913 --> 00:07:48,479
and there was Tyler,

239
00:07:48,479 --> 00:07:50,201
twenty-four years old,

240
00:07:50,201 --> 00:07:52,550
with a duffel bag and no coat,

241
00:07:52,550 --> 00:07:53,176
shaking.

242
00:07:53,356 --> 00:07:55,010
He said, Grandma,

243
00:07:55,010 --> 00:07:58,415
I'm sorry, I didn't know where else

244
00:07:58,415 --> 00:07:58,999
to go.

245
00:07:59,179 --> 00:07:59,969
And I said,

246
00:07:59,969 --> 00:08:02,339
the only thing I'm sorry about is

247
00:08:02,339 --> 00:08:04,206
that you waited this long.

248
00:08:04,386 --> 00:08:05,640
Get in here.

249
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:08,269
I made him a grilled cheese and

250
00:08:08,269 --> 00:08:09,887
a bowl of tomato soup,

251
00:08:09,887 --> 00:08:11,284
at eleven at night,

252
00:08:11,284 --> 00:08:13,343
and he ate it like he hadn't

253
00:08:13,343 --> 00:08:14,372
eaten in days.

254
00:08:14,552 --> 00:08:15,910
Maybe he hadn't.

255
00:08:16,090 --> 00:08:18,203
And he told me everything,

256
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:19,829
at my kitchen table.

257
00:08:20,009 --> 00:08:21,880
The job, Brianna,

258
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:22,760
the car.

259
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:24,682
He cried, honey.

260
00:08:24,862 --> 00:08:26,197
A grown man.

261
00:08:26,377 --> 00:08:28,381
I hadn't seen him cry since he

262
00:08:28,381 --> 00:08:30,185
fell off his bike at seven.

263
00:08:30,365 --> 00:08:32,343
I made up the spare room,

264
00:08:32,343 --> 00:08:34,638
the one with the cedar chest.

265
00:08:34,988 --> 00:08:37,279
The house was cold that night,

266
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:39,569
because I keep the heat low to

267
00:08:39,569 --> 00:08:40,409
save money.

268
00:08:40,589 --> 00:08:42,002
And in the morning,

269
00:08:42,002 --> 00:08:44,975
Tyler came down to breakfast wearing the

270
00:08:44,975 --> 00:08:46,313
navy blue sweater.

271
00:08:46,493 --> 00:08:47,792
He'd been freezing,

272
00:08:47,792 --> 00:08:50,459
and he'd opened the cedar chest looking

273
00:08:50,459 --> 00:08:51,416
for a blanket.

274
00:08:51,596 --> 00:08:52,844
And there it was,

275
00:08:52,844 --> 00:08:54,312
in the tissue paper,

276
00:08:54,312 --> 00:08:56,147
with the tag still on it.

277
00:08:56,327 --> 00:08:58,463
Finally, you're big enough.

278
00:08:58,643 --> 00:09:00,869
He stood in my kitchen doorway,

279
00:09:00,869 --> 00:09:03,310
wearing it, and he couldn't speak.

280
00:09:03,661 --> 00:09:05,584
It fit him perfectly,

281
00:09:05,584 --> 00:09:06,134
honey.

282
00:09:06,314 --> 00:09:07,986
He'd grown into it,

283
00:09:07,986 --> 00:09:09,483
just like I said.

284
00:09:09,663 --> 00:09:11,891
He sat down at the table and

285
00:09:11,891 --> 00:09:14,516
said, Grandma, I was such a jerk.

286
00:09:14,696 --> 00:09:16,182
I'm so sorry.

287
00:09:16,362 --> 00:09:17,338
And I said,

288
00:09:17,338 --> 00:09:18,847
you were fifteen.

289
00:09:19,027 --> 00:09:22,045
Fifteen-year-olds are supposed to be jerks.

290
00:09:22,225 --> 00:09:24,466
It's in the Bible somewhere.

291
00:09:24,646 --> 00:09:25,817
And he laughed,

292
00:09:25,817 --> 00:09:28,471
the first time I'd heard him laugh

293
00:09:28,471 --> 00:09:29,174
in years.

294
00:09:29,354 --> 00:09:31,616
And then he put his hand on

295
00:09:31,616 --> 00:09:33,207
his chest and said,

296
00:09:33,207 --> 00:09:33,626
wait.

297
00:09:33,806 --> 00:09:35,223
What's this?

298
00:09:35,573 --> 00:09:38,296
He'd felt the little tape inside the

299
00:09:38,296 --> 00:09:40,263
hem, right over his heart.

300
00:09:40,443 --> 00:09:42,962
I handed him my sewing scissors.

301
00:09:43,142 --> 00:09:45,116
He snipped three stitches,

302
00:09:45,116 --> 00:09:48,305
very carefully, and pulled out that little

303
00:09:48,305 --> 00:09:49,899
strip of cotton tape.

304
00:09:50,079 --> 00:09:52,406
And he read the seven words,

305
00:09:52,406 --> 00:09:53,569
in red thread,

306
00:09:53,569 --> 00:09:54,317
out loud.

307
00:09:54,497 --> 00:09:57,852
Whatever happens, you can always come home.

308
00:09:58,032 --> 00:09:59,785
He read it twice.

309
00:09:59,965 --> 00:10:02,000
Then he put his head down on

310
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,235
my kitchen table,

311
00:10:03,235 --> 00:10:04,760
honey, and he sobbed.

312
00:10:05,111 --> 00:10:06,471
He said, Grandma,

313
00:10:06,471 --> 00:10:08,872
you wrote this nine years ago.

314
00:10:09,052 --> 00:10:10,445
You didn't know.

315
00:10:10,625 --> 00:10:11,666
And I said,

316
00:10:11,666 --> 00:10:14,410
honey, I didn't need to know.

317
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:18,229
Grandmas always know somebody's going to need

318
00:10:18,229 --> 00:10:19,118
it someday.

319
00:10:19,298 --> 00:10:22,190
I've stitched those same seven words inside

320
00:10:22,190 --> 00:10:24,140
every sweater I've ever made.

321
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:27,636
Every grandchild of mine who's turned fifteen

322
00:10:27,636 --> 00:10:29,625
has that tape in their hem.

323
00:10:29,805 --> 00:10:32,046
Most of them have never found it.

324
00:10:32,226 --> 00:10:35,140
Tyler's the first one who needed to.

325
00:10:35,490 --> 00:10:38,317
Tyler stayed with me for seven months,

326
00:10:38,317 --> 00:10:39,135
sweetheart.

327
00:10:39,315 --> 00:10:41,317
He found a job at a hardware

328
00:10:41,317 --> 00:10:43,390
store, and then a better one.

329
00:10:43,570 --> 00:10:46,420
He fixed everything in my house that

330
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,924
Harold used to fix.

331
00:10:48,104 --> 00:10:50,066
And one night in January,

332
00:10:50,066 --> 00:10:52,970
he picked up my knitting needles from

333
00:10:52,970 --> 00:10:54,540
the basket and said,

334
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:55,952
Grandma, teach me.

335
00:10:56,132 --> 00:10:58,872
His big hands were so clumsy.

336
00:10:59,052 --> 00:11:01,482
His first scarf had more holes than

337
00:11:01,482 --> 00:11:02,454
a fishing net.

338
00:11:02,634 --> 00:11:04,178
He wore it anyway.

339
00:11:04,358 --> 00:11:05,403
Every day.

340
00:11:05,753 --> 00:11:08,815
That Christmas, Tyler wore the navy sweater

341
00:11:08,815 --> 00:11:10,025
to dinner, honey.

342
00:11:10,205 --> 00:11:11,104
Susan saw it,

343
00:11:11,104 --> 00:11:13,110
and she put her hand over her

344
00:11:13,110 --> 00:11:13,526
mouth.

345
00:11:13,706 --> 00:11:16,181
The cousins were all grown up by

346
00:11:16,181 --> 00:11:17,883
then, and one of them,

347
00:11:17,883 --> 00:11:19,740
the one who'd snickered,

348
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:22,448
said, hey, I remember that sweater.

349
00:11:22,628 --> 00:11:24,326
And Tyler said,

350
00:11:24,326 --> 00:11:24,892
yeah.

351
00:11:25,072 --> 00:11:27,022
Grandma made it for me.

352
00:11:27,202 --> 00:11:28,932
Best thing I own.

353
00:11:29,112 --> 00:11:31,315
And he looked right at me across

354
00:11:31,315 --> 00:11:32,003
the table.

355
00:11:32,183 --> 00:11:33,983
Nobody laughed this time.

356
00:11:34,333 --> 00:11:36,535
He has his own apartment now,

357
00:11:36,535 --> 00:11:38,205
honey, and a good job,

358
00:11:38,205 --> 00:11:41,319
and a sweet girlfriend who teaches second

359
00:11:41,319 --> 00:11:41,775
grade.

360
00:11:41,955 --> 00:11:43,270
And last month,

361
00:11:43,270 --> 00:11:45,902
he finished his first sweater.

362
00:11:46,082 --> 00:11:46,950
A tiny one,

363
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:50,029
for his girlfriend's sister's new baby.

364
00:11:50,209 --> 00:11:52,372
And before he sewed up the hem,

365
00:11:52,372 --> 00:11:54,396
he called me on the phone and

366
00:11:54,396 --> 00:11:56,837
asked me how to stitch letters with

367
00:11:56,837 --> 00:11:57,605
red thread.

368
00:11:57,785 --> 00:11:59,556
I didn't have to ask what he

369
00:11:59,556 --> 00:12:00,757
was going to write.

370
00:12:01,107 --> 00:12:03,921
Here's what I want you to remember,

371
00:12:03,921 --> 00:12:04,404
honey.

372
00:12:04,584 --> 00:12:07,334
Keep the gift your grandmother made you,

373
00:12:07,334 --> 00:12:08,984
even if you laugh at it.

374
00:12:09,164 --> 00:12:12,717
Someday you'll understand what's stitched inside.

375
00:12:12,897 --> 00:12:14,771
Tell me in the comments,

376
00:12:14,771 --> 00:12:18,284
sweetheart, what's the handmade gift you wish

377
00:12:18,284 --> 00:12:19,143
you'd kept?

378
00:12:19,323 --> 00:12:21,628
There are more of my family's stories

379
00:12:21,628 --> 00:12:23,809
in the playlist right there on your

380
00:12:23,809 --> 00:12:24,245
screen.

381
00:12:24,425 --> 00:12:25,319
Good night.

382
00:12:25,499 --> 00:12:26,986
Merry Christmas.
